    "rich_text_guide": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "guide_h": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How to Choose Cookware",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "guide_t": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "enlarge_text": false,
            "text": "<p><strong>Stainless steel</strong> (Hestan ProBond and NanoBond) is the all-purpose workhorse. It sears well, builds a pan sauce, goes in the oven and lasts for decades.</p><p><strong>Cast iron and carbon steel</strong> (Lodge and Smithey) hold heat for a hard sear and get more nonstick with every use. They go from stovetop to oven to grill.</p><p><strong>Enameled cast iron</strong> (Le Creuset) needs no seasoning and is made for braises, soups, stews and bread, with color that can go straight to the table.</p><p><strong>Nonstick</strong> makes eggs, fish and weeknight cleanup easy. Cook on low to medium heat and it will last longer.</p><p>Most kitchens start with three pieces: a 10\" to 12\" <a href=\"/collections/fry-pans-skillets\">skillet</a>, a 2 to 3 qt. <a href=\"/collections/saucepans\">saucepan</a> and a 5 to 7 qt. <a href=\"/collections/dutch-ovens-braisers\">Dutch oven</a> or <a href=\"/collections/stockpots\">stockpot</a>. A <a href=\"/collections/cookware-sets\">cookware set</a> covers all of them in one purchase.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "guide_h",
        "guide_t"
      ],
      "name": "t:labels.rich_text",
      "settings": {
        "align_text": "left",
